# CLI reference

The `pontia` CLI configures the local installation and delegates durable control-plane operations to the running `pontiad` service.

Use `pontia COMMAND --help` for the exact options in your installed version.

## Setup and lifecycle

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `pontia init` | Select Agent Clients, create persistent configuration, install integrations, start the per-user service, and offer remote setup |
| `pontia setup [CLIENT]` | Install or repair recommended local integrations; select Clients when omitted; enable the selected Clients |
| `pontia up` | Install or refresh the per-user service definition and start or restart the service |
| `pontia status` | Show the data directory, local service health, signed-in user, remote handle, and selected edge |
| `pontia doctor` | Explain service, configuration, login, and remote-registration checks, with repair hints |
| `pontia down` | Stop and disable the per-user service |
| `pontia update` | Install the latest signed stable `pontia` and `pontiad` release |
| `pontia auto-update enable` | Install and start six-hour automatic update checks |
| `pontia auto-update disable` | Stop and remove the automatic update schedule |
| `pontia auto-update status` | Show the schedule and available run times |
| `pontia uninstall` | Remove Pontia integrations, shell configuration, and autostart services |
| `pontia --version` | Print the installed version |
| `pontia completions SHELL` | Print the completion script for a shell |

If Pontia-started agents are running, `pontia down` and `pontia uninstall` list their Sessions. Use `--force` to stop those agents and continue; this check also works when the service is offline. The command reports an older Codex background server separately when you can manage it from Codex.

`pontia uninstall` shows the cleanup list and asks for confirmation; use `--yes` to skip the prompt. It keeps the binaries, configuration, credentials, history, cloud registration, and Agent Client installations. Open a new terminal after cleanup. Run `pontia init` to set up again.

`pontia setup` accepts `pi`, `codex`, or `claude` and applies the recommended configuration without additional Client options.

See [Initialization](/docs/initialization) for interactive setup and the equivalent step-by-step command sequence.

Stopping `pontiad` stops observation, input reception, and Workflow scheduling. Agent Clients manage the lifecycle of their native conversations separately.

## Bot commands

Bot tools run inside a Pontia Bot, helper, retrieval, or Dreaming Session. Add the global `--json` option for machine-readable output.

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `pontia bot send-to-user` | Send a complete reply, link it to earlier messages, open a todo, or resolve todos |
| `pontia bot link-message` | Link an unquoted user message to one to five earlier messages |
| `pontia bot todo mark` | Mark an existing chat message as an open todo |
| `pontia bot todo resolve` | Resolve a todo on an existing chat message |
| `pontia bot set-description` | Fill the current Bot's empty description |
| `pontia bot update-user` | Replace text in the shared `USER.md`, or append with an empty `--old-text` |
| `pontia bot chat-history --query TEXT` | Search this Bot's chat for literal text, files, or images |
| `pontia bot chat-history context --message-id ID` | Read a matched chat message with its chronological neighbors |
| `pontia bot session-messages` | Read a target Session's paginated messages by Turn and category |
| `pontia bot helper create` | Start a general or Profile-based helper in an active or temporary Workspace |
| `pontia bot helper list` | List the current Bot's open helper Sessions |
| `pontia bot helper send` | Send additional instructions to a helper |
| `pontia bot helper reply` | Report a helper result to its Bot |
| `pontia bot helper check` | Read helper status, capabilities, and paginated messages |
| `pontia bot helper compact` | Request context compaction for an idle or interrupted helper |
| `pontia bot helper close` | Exit a helper Session while retaining its history |
| `pontia bot profile templates` | Fetch recommended Agent Profile templates |
| `pontia bot profile list` | List shared Profiles and their availability |
| `pontia bot profile show` | Read one Profile, including its system prompt |
| `pontia bot profile clients` | List enabled Clients available to Profiles |
| `pontia bot profile models` | List models offered by a Client |
| `pontia bot profile create` | Create a shared Agent Profile |
| `pontia bot profile update` | Change selected Profile fields |
| `pontia bot profile delete` | Delete a shared Profile |
| `pontia bot workspace register` | Register and activate a directory within a configured root |
| `pontia bot workspace describe` | Set or clear a Workspace description |
| `pontia bot memory pending` | List active memories awaiting organization |
| `pontia bot memory organize` | Mark memory organization complete or pending |
| `pontia bot memory create` | Create a device memory |
| `pontia bot memory update` | Replace a memory's content and metadata |
| `pontia bot memory archive` | Archive or restore a memory |
| `pontia bot memory link` | Link two active memories |
| `pontia bot memory unlink` | Remove a link between memories |
| `pontia bot memory read` | Read active memories by ID |
| `pontia bot memory search` | Search memories by whole keywords |
| `pontia bot memory retrieve` | Start a read-only deep-retrieval helper |
| `pontia bot memory neighbors` | Read one hop of linked active memories |
| `pontia bot memory use` | Record memories used in a response or decision |
| `pontia bot dreaming clarify` | Ask a Bot to clarify ambiguous memories |

Memory writes performed by Dreaming use the global `--reason` option to record their evidence. Paginated `session-messages`, `helper check`, `memory pending`, and `memory neighbors` results print the next command to run. `helper check` also reports context usage and manual-compaction availability.

## Shell completion

`pontia completions` supports `bash`, `zsh`, `fish`, `elvish`, and `powershell`. Add the line for your shell to its configuration file, then open a new terminal:

```sh
# bash: ~/.bashrc
source <(pontia completions bash)

# zsh: ~/.zshrc, after compinit
source <(pontia completions zsh)

# fish: run once
mkdir -p ~/.config/fish/completions
pontia completions fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/pontia.fish

# elvish: ~/.config/elvish/rc.elv
eval (pontia completions elvish | slurp)
```

```powershell
# PowerShell: $PROFILE
pontia completions powershell | Out-String | Invoke-Expression
```

## Agent Clients

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `pontia client list` | List every Agent Client entry with its short ID, name, type, and enabled state |
| `pontia client list --json` | List every entry as JSON, including its ID |
| `pontia client install --list` | Show registered installers, methods, detection results, and prerequisites |
| `pontia client install CLIENT` | Install a Client, apply its integration setup, and enable it |
| `pontia client show CLIENT` | Show one entry |
| `pontia client enable CLIENT` | Allow new Sessions to use the entry |
| `pontia client disable CLIENT` | Stop new Sessions from using the entry; existing Sessions are unchanged |
| `pontia client reset CLIENT` | Delete the entry's saved settings, which also disables it |

`CLIENT` is a short ID such as `pi`, `codex`, `claude-agent`, or `grok`. Use `--id ID` with an ID from `pontia client list --json` when a short ID is ambiguous.

## Agent tools

`pontia --help` lists `workflow` and `bot` under Agent tools.

### Workflows

| Command | Context | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `pontia workflow run FILE` | Pontia Session | Parse a TOML definition and start a Workflow through the local API |
| `pontia workflow show [WORKFLOW_ID]` | Any local shell; ID optional inside a managed Node | Show progress, the initiating Session, current Node, inputs, and output |
| `pontia workflow submit` | Current Agent Node Session | Declare the Node's output ready |
| `pontia workflow terminate` | Current Agent Node Session | Send the blocking reason to the initiating Session |
| `pontia workflow retry ID FAILURE_EVENT_ID` | Pontia Session | Retry the indicated failure in the same Node Session |

## Cloud login and remote access

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `pontia login` | Complete browser-based device authorization and store a CLI credential |
| `pontia remote edges` | List available edges as a JSON array of IDs and names |
| `pontia remote enable` | Register this device, select an edge interactively, and create its E2E identity |
| `pontia remote enable --edge EDGE_ID --name NAME` | Register without prompts; both options are required |
| `pontia remote edit` | Edit the name, handle, and Edge interactively; Enter keeps each current value |
| `pontia remote edit --name NAME` | Change the device display name |
| `pontia remote edit --handle HANDLE` | Change the device Dashboard URL handle |
| `pontia remote rotate-key` | Replace the device E2E identity and key version |
| `pontia remote disable` | Unregister this device and remove its local remote identity |

Remote configuration changes that affect `pontiad` restart a running managed service. See [switching Edge](/docs/device-management#switch-edge) for stopped services and recovery.

## Exit status and errors

`pontia status` and `pontia doctor` exit successfully when the local service is running and its HTTP health check passes. Account and remote results report separate stages; use the remote reachability result to confirm internet access to the device.

Use the exit status to check command success. Errors are written to standard error with a `pontia:` prefix. Include the redacted error when diagnosing a failure.

## `pontia-edge`

A self-hosted Edge has a separate root-operated CLI:

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `pontia-edge init --edge-id ID --ticket TICKET` | Initialize and register an Edge; normally use the full Cloud-generated command |
| `pontia-edge retry` | Resume incomplete initialization on the same server |
| `pontia-edge update` | Install the latest signed stable Edge release |
| `pontia-edge auto-update enable` | Install and enable update checks every 10 minutes |
| `pontia-edge auto-update disable` | Disable future checks |
| `pontia-edge auto-update status` | Show timer schedule and the last update result |

See [Self-host an Edge](/docs/self-hosted-edge) before using these commands directly.
